    sectionType: "explain",
    title: "What Does This Mean?",
    content: "Hebrews 9:25-28 addresses one of the most consequential theological questions surrounding the Lord's Supper: Is Christ's sacrifice repeated in the communion service? The writer's answer is unequivocal: Christ does not offer Himself \"often.\" He appeared \"once at the end of the ages\" to put away sin by the sacrifice of Himself. The word hapax — once, once for all — is the controlling concept. Any theology of the Lord's Supper that treats the service as a re-offering or continuation of Christ's sacrifice contradicts the finished work declared in Hebrews. The cross was complete. Communion remembers what was accomplished; it does not add to it.\n\nJohn 6:53-58 has been the subject of intense debate across Christian history. Jesus' language — \"eat My flesh,\" \"drink My blood\" — shocked His original audience and continues to generate controversy. Some traditions read these verses as a mandate for the literal consumption of Christ's body and blood in the Eucharist. But the context resists that interpretation. In John 6:63, Jesus explicitly states: \"It is the Spirit who gives life; the flesh profits nothing.\" The eating and drinking He describes is the act of believing — receiving Him by faith, depending on Him for life, as surely as the body depends on food. To \"eat\" Christ is to trust Him so completely that His life becomes the sustaining reality of yours.\n\n1 Corinthians 11:17-22 exposes a different kind of misunderstanding — not theological but behavioral. The Corinthians were turning the Lord's Supper into a private dinner party. The wealthy arrived early and ate their fill; the poor arrived late and went hungry. Paul is blunt: \"it is not to eat the Lord's Supper.\" When the service becomes an occasion for social division, selfishness, or disregard for the body, it ceases to be the Lord's Supper at all, regardless of what the participants call it. The name on the event is irrelevant if the character of Christ is absent from the practice.\n\nThese three passages together guard the Lord's Supper from three persistent errors: re-sacrificing Christ (Hebrews 9), materializing the elements (John 6), and privatizing the practice (1 Corinthians 11). Each error distorts the supper's purpose: to remember, to commune, and to unite.",
